r  best way out of this pandemic to  move into an endemic we're not there yet  but we want everyone who can to receive  the vaccination we understand people  have a right to protest  but as long as it's not breaking any  laws or  causing damage  it's not  spreading  language that's not appropriate so we  are watching very carefully we're seeing  we've seen what's happened in ottawa we  witnessed what was happening in toronto  last weekend and again as long as it's  peaceful protest that's fine but it  can't be destructive and it also can't  block hospitals  either for healthcare staff to access  hospitals or for people to receive  treatment  thank you david um the next one is the  senior vice president of uh hallmark if  i can ask her a question if possible  how concerned is walmart over the border  closures and the uh demonstrations how  long will it take before it affects  what's on the shelves  i think in terms of the border closures  i think you know like myself we watch it  every night and it's certainly a concern  for all canadians  and we'll leave it to the federal  government to uh to address and see if  we can move things forward i think what  i would say on behalf of walmart we're  happy to be here today to support the  minister and support the province around  moving the province forward  in this pandemic  we'll now go to the phone lines for  questions over to the first question  please  your first question comes from holly  mackenzie suiter with the canadian press  please go ahead  hi holly hi minister i wanted to follow  up about the question about other  provinces dropping restrictions like  alberta and saskatchewan specifically  are looking to drop vaccine passports  and masks uh pretty quickly so i'm just  wondering if ontario  is looking to do the same like planning  to to adjust its plans at all based on  changing indicators or  if you just have any more information  about how the reopening timeline might  change specifically around those  vaccination and mask measures  well we've already set out our opening  timelines we started on january 31st the  next phase is as of february 21st and  then march 14th  we have no plans currently to drop the  passport  vaccination  situation or masking  we believe that masking is going to be  important for some time to come and of  course we take our  advice from  dr moore the chief medical officer of  health and the people at the science  advisory table and other  medical experts and so we always said  that we were going to take a very  cautious  phased prudent approach to opening up  and that's the path that we're going to  follow  follow up  hi yeah i'm wondering as well about the  rapid test results because we know  quebec is trying to track those in a  centralized way  and i'm wondering if ontario is going to  try and do a similar thing now that  you're making the test more available to  the public  and if you're not planning to try and  track the results can you explain why  well they're not going to be entered  into the kovac system but what we're  taking a look at is more of the extent  of  circulating in the community and that  comes to us from a variety of ways one  is the number of people who are  hospitalized that doesn't tell the  entire picture of course because we  don't have all of the results of the  rapid test but it is a quite a good  indicator of what's happening in the  community we also have wastewater  surveillance that gives us some  indication of what's happening and the  number of people in icu's there's a  number of different other indicators  that we're looking at that tells us when  it's  safe  for us to ease further public health  restrictions  next question  your next question comes from brian  lilly with the toronto sun please go  ahead hi brian  good morning mister um  i i know that you said you you have laid  out your plan but your plan  going up to march 14th makes no mention  of when we might see a removal of  vaccine passports or  masks 