le we have the  variant the ba2 in some parts of ontario  as well while it doesn't appear as if  it's going to be a more dangerous  variant than omicron it certainly  appears to be more transmissible so we  still need to be very careful uh we are  not telling the people of ontario that  this is going to remain in place forever  no but we're not in the clear just yet  and so we need to continue to  protect ontarians protect each other  with the with the passports and with the  masks at this point  next question  your next question comes from lorenda  rudikoff with cbc please go ahead hi  lorenda  hi there minister  with these multiple protests in  different parts of the province  including now at a major border crossing  we're not hearing from the premier where  is he and why isn't he speaking about  this  the premier is working around the clock  to work on  helping to  open up our economy to make sure that  people get their vaccinations he is  working hard with us day to day  and i know that this is a matter of  concern to him but we also need to work  with the federal government too when  we're  border crossings and so we ask the  federal government to uh to assist with  this as well  follow-up  on the rapid test the last time that  these were made available we saw huge  lineups and then in some cases we saw  people selling them later on the black  market what are you doing to ensure  that that doesn't happen this time  well first of all we know that the  organizations the pharmacies and the  stores that are offering  the rapid test some of them have their  own uh systems for  ordering some people have to order  online and then pick up in store so that  will give them the opportunity to  make sure that they don't have too many  people coming in too often to receive  the tests but we also know that we're in  a much different situation this time  than last time because of the quantities  of the tests that we're receiving  last time it was a total of two million  tasks and that were  taken from the shelves very very quickly  now we have five and a half million  tests per week that are available to  people and we know that most ontarians  want to have the test there's five tests  in each box we they know that they want  to protect their family and need them  for specific purposes and that they will  do the right thing but we also have the  ability to find people that are  obtaining tests that they don't need for  their own purposes and trying to sell  them online but with the vast quantities  available now  it wouldn't appear that there's going to  be much of a secondary market because  people can obtain them quite readily  last question  your final question comes from jeff gray  with the globe and mail please go ahead  oh hi thanks minister i want to ask  about uh directive 2  these graphs from the science table  yesterday that looked at the sort of  sustained impact  of omicron on the hospital even with a  more optimistic  uh level of vaccination still show you  know  two thousand to four thousand patients  uh well through march and several  hundred in the icu are you confident the  hospitals can  reasonably get back to business as  normal even given the fact that they  also will have this backlog to be trying  to fill  yes we know uh first of all that many  people have had  surgeries and procedures postponed some  for very long periods of time and we  want to be able to get back to be able  to perform their surgeries for them i  know this is very concerning to many  people especially if they're waiting for  a cancer or cardiac surgery but many  other people are waiting for orthopedic  surgeries and  and other surgeries as well so this is  something we want to get back to as  quickly as possible one thing that i  would note is that our government has  brought in 3 100 extra beds at a cost of  over 5 billion dollars since the  beginning of the pandemic those beds are  going to stay  because we need to  help people that have been