s and that were  taken from the shelves very very quickly  now we have five and a half million  tests per week that are available to  people and we know that most ontarians  want to have the test there's five tests  in each box we they know that they want  to protect their family and need them  for specific purposes and that they will  do the right thing but we also have the  ability to find people that are  obtaining tests that they don't need for  their own purposes and trying to sell  them online but with the vast quantities  available now  it wouldn't appear that there's going to  be much of a secondary market because  people can obtain them quite readily  last question  your final question comes from jeff gray  with the globe and mail please go ahead  oh hi thanks minister i want to ask  about uh directive 2  these graphs from the science table  yesterday that looked at the sort of  sustained impact  of omicron on the hospital even with a  more optimistic  uh level of vaccination still show you  know  two thousand to four thousand patients  uh well through march and several  hundred in the icu are you confident the  hospitals can  reasonably get back to business as  normal even given the fact that they  also will have this backlog to be trying  to fill  yes we know uh first of all that many  people have had  surgeries and procedures postponed some  for very long periods of time and we  want to be able to get back to be able  to perform their surgeries for them i  know this is very concerning to many  people especially if they're waiting for  a cancer or cardiac surgery but many  other people are waiting for orthopedic  surgeries and  and other surgeries as well so this is  something we want to get back to as  quickly as possible one thing that i  would note is that our government has  brought in 3 100 extra beds at a cost of  over 5 billion dollars since the  beginning of the pandemic those beds are  going to stay  because we need to  help people that have been waiting for  these procedures and yes i do feel  confident that we will have the capacity  in our hospitals to help them as soon as  possible we are looking at lifting  directive 2  very soon and i know that ontario health  and uh  others are working at lifting this chief  medical officer of health are looking at  lifting this as soon as possible so we  can get back to  all of those surgeries that many  ontarians have been waiting long periods  of time for  follow-up and this is the last question  thanks minister just and i just wanted  to ask a a sort of clarification about  your remarks off the top you mentioned  about the rapid test that uh you said  something defected this kind of thing  but in the uh  technical briefing it seems the official  guidance from  the ministry is to not use the rapid  tests on  asymptomatic people before a social  gathering can you maybe clarify your  remarks that what you meant by  being able to go out to dinner and so on  sure well there may be situations where  people want to see  a senior family member who may be very  vulnerable maybe immunocompromised may  have other concerns and so it would be  safe for the family to be tested in  those situations but not just to go out  and get a test because you want to go to  a party that's not what this is intended  for this is intended to be used to  protect  vulnerable people in our community  thanks everyone thank you
 

